The regen ran as `smart-test --manifest >/dev/null 2>&1`, so every non-zero
exit -- hypergumbo not installed, the slice crashing, or WI-fopuh's refusal to
write the manifest of a repo the caller is not in -- arrived as one fixed line:

    ⚠️  Manifest generation skipped (no stable hypergumbo?)

One message standing in for several causes is this project's absent-versus-empty
defect at the diagnostics layer. It is not silence; it is a confident wrong
answer, and it leaves no route back to the real one. The parenthetical names the
first of the three, and is wrong for the case that now fires on every
tests/test_autopr_*.py fixture run.

The skip now reports the exit status and relays smart-test's own stderr,
indented beneath it:

    ⚠️  Manifest generation skipped (smart-test exited 3):
       ERROR: smart-test belongs to /home/…/hypergumbo,
              but the current directory is inside /tmp/…/fakerepo.
       …

The status is captured as the FIRST statement of the branch, because any command
there would overwrite $?, and it is the one piece of evidence stderr cannot
restate. An empty stderr is reported as the distinct fact it is -- the only case
where the old guess was defensible.

No behaviour change: the regen is still best-effort, still non-fatal, and runs
exactly when it ran before.

Six tests. Four pin the relay, the status, the removed guess, and the composed
end-to-end path against the real smart-test; two are difference arms that pass
before the change and must keep passing -- a successful regen is not reported as
skipped, and an explicit AUTO_PR_SKIP_MANIFEST=1 does not borrow the failure
line. They copy scripts/ AND .githooks/ into a throwaway tree so the stub
smart-test's exit code and stderr are the test's to choose; AUTO_PR_SIMULATE_OUTAGE
keeps the run off the network, because a test that passes because a push fails
fast is a flake with a good day.

419 passed across the auto-pr/CI/smart-test slice, 2337 across the full affected
slice.

